So another way, the second
way, of building research

253
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:52,100
during products
is actually start

254
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:54,380
with a deep belief
in what you want

255
00:13:54,380 --> 00:14:00,710
to make either from a product
perspective or kind of vision.

256
00:14:00,710 --> 00:14:03,480
And literally, make
the model do that.

257
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:08,090
So I feel like this is
more of a common thing.

258
00:14:08,090 --> 00:14:12,170
So we can go through
some examples.

259
00:14:12,170 --> 00:14:14,720
Before Anthropic, I worked
at The New York Times.

260
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:17,540
And in a lot of ways,
we were thinking

261
00:14:17,540 --> 00:14:21,020
about how to represent
information to people.

262
00:14:21,020 --> 00:14:23,510
Like, how do we add
layer of context

263
00:14:23,510 --> 00:14:25,020
to the product and coverage.

264
00:14:25,020 --> 00:14:27,570
And at that time, we were
working on elections.

265
00:14:27,570 --> 00:14:30,690
And we only had tools like NLP.

266
00:14:30,690 --> 00:14:33,470
But you can imagine
this idea, this concept

267
00:14:33,470 --> 00:14:36,510
could have extended, given
the current tools of AI,

268
00:14:36,510 --> 00:14:39,340
to have much more dynamic--

269
00:14:39,340 --> 00:14:43,690
representation of dynamic
UIs that people could

270
00:14:43,690 --> 00:14:46,450
consume the content better.

271
00:14:46,450 --> 00:14:51,520
Same when I was working on
this product, the command

272
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:53,500
line, the terminal,
the new terminal--

273
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:55,420
I think it would be
much, much richer

274
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:58,720
if you could have integrated
autocompletion or some

275
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:03,940
of the benefits of GPT-3 at that
time into the product itself.

276
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:05,740
And so we wanted to have--

277
00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:09,130
it was a vision of having
much more humane command

278
00:15:09,130 --> 00:15:15,880
line for junior engineers to
actually be more well equipped.

279
00:15:15,880 --> 00:15:18,490
And then early
prototypes around--

280
00:15:18,490 --> 00:15:22,720
with GPT-3, even though it was
just next-token prediction,

281
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:27,650
how do I make a
writing IDE with GPT-3?

282
00:15:27,650 --> 00:15:31,690
And at that time, it
was just trying to--

283
00:15:31,690 --> 00:15:35,150
whenever I type, it would
autocomplete my thoughts almost.

284
00:15:35,150 --> 00:15:38,400
So those ideas
present early days

285
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:41,530
when the technology
was coming out.

286
00:15:41,530 --> 00:15:44,260
And I feel like when I was at
Anthropic, I realized that.

287
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:47,640
And actually, if you want
to create new interaction

288
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:50,130
paradigm of interfaces,
you actually

289
00:15:50,130 --> 00:15:53,850
need to train the
model for that.

290
00:15:53,850 --> 00:15:57,070
Another example of what
we did with Claude,

291
00:15:57,070 --> 00:16:00,000
which I don't think
a lot of people know,

292
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,210
is when Claude generates
titles, it actually

293
00:16:03,210 --> 00:16:05,410
has some micro personalization.

294
00:16:05,410 --> 00:16:09,570
So it takes the writing
style of the user

295
00:16:09,570 --> 00:16:12,550
and then generates a title in
the same style of the user.

296
00:16:12,550 --> 00:16:15,540
So you can imagine
like interesting micro

297
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:17,880
personalization that
you could create

298
00:16:17,880 --> 00:16:19,690
within the products themselves.

299
00:16:19,690 --> 00:16:26,050
And another project
was Claude in Slack.

300
00:16:26,050 --> 00:16:28,590
And that was the vision
of Claude becoming

301
00:16:28,590 --> 00:16:29,980
a first virtual teammate.

302
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:32,670
It was back in 2022.

303
00:16:32,670 --> 00:16:36,980
And Slack was a very,
very natural workspace

304
00:16:36,980 --> 00:16:38,120
where people collaborate.

305
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:43,340
And you can imagine
a Claude model

306
00:16:43,340 --> 00:16:47,100
to be able to jump into the
threads and suggest new things.

307
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:50,600
Or sometimes, Claude
was really, really good

308
00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:55,520
at summarizing what was
going on within this channel

309
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:57,830
at the high volume content.

310
00:16:57,830 --> 00:17:00,260
And so this was the
first kind of vision

311
00:17:00,260 --> 00:17:02,930
and the first attempt
of making Claude being

312
00:17:02,930 --> 00:17:05,690
a virtual super
assistant and being

313
00:17:05,690 --> 00:17:08,690
able to use different tools.

314
00:17:08,690 --> 00:17:15,079
And one of the first projects
at OpenAI was around canvas.

315
00:17:15,079 --> 00:17:19,790
So that was in the same
spirit of breaking out

316
00:17:19,790 --> 00:17:22,500
from the chat interface
to something different.

317
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:27,839
And we wanted to create a much
more human-AI, collaborative,

318
00:17:27,839 --> 00:17:32,010
and flexible affordance that
will scale with new modalities.

319
00:17:32,010 --> 00:17:35,490
So canvas is not
just like a thing

320
00:17:35,490 --> 00:17:36,970
that you can write into this.

321
00:17:36,970 --> 00:17:40,150
It's also a thing that the
model can write into this.

322
00:17:40,150 --> 00:17:42,340
And the model can render code.

323
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:43,980
And another model can check.

324
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:46,290
And you can create
the interfaces that

325
00:17:46,290 --> 00:17:49,380
will scale with new
model capabilities

326
00:17:49,380 --> 00:17:52,500
and other tools
that will happen.

327
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:56,340
So interesting
things about Canvas

328
00:17:56,340 --> 00:17:59,160
is that we actually
post-trained the model purely

329
00:17:59,160 --> 00:18:00,430
on synthetic data.

330
00:18:00,430 --> 00:18:08,820
And we live in the age where
most powerful reasoning models

331
00:18:08,820 --> 00:18:11,110
can be distilled via APIs.

332
00:18:11,110 --> 00:18:15,180
And this distillation
is a very powerful idea

333
00:18:15,180 --> 00:18:20,160
of having a student teacher and
have a teacher to teach things

334
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:21,040
to a smaller model.

335
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:25,390
So we've trained this model to
become more of a collaborator.

336
00:18:25,390 --> 00:18:28,120
But what does it mean for a
model to become a collaborator?

337
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:31,884
And how do we actually
make evals for this?

338
00:18:31,884 --> 00:18:36,530
So one thing that we
wanted to decompose

339
00:18:36,530 --> 00:18:40,280
is that teaching the
model to use a tool

340
00:18:40,280 --> 00:18:43,310
is a different
behavior from having

341
00:18:43,310 --> 00:18:47,190
a model to be proactive
or act as a collaborator.

342
00:18:47,190 --> 00:18:50,480
And so teaching a
tool in itself also

343
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:52,620
have a nuanced kind of behavior.

344
00:18:52,620 --> 00:18:57,230
So one thing that we had
to calibrate the model on

345
00:18:57,230 --> 00:19:02,090
is when to entirely rewrite
the document versus when to--

346
00:19:02,090 --> 00:19:04,370
if you look at the canvas,
sometimes the model

347
00:19:04,370 --> 00:19:08,420
can just specifically select
sections and delete those

348
00:19:08,420 --> 00:19:11,150
and rewrite them in a
much more fine grained way

349
00:19:11,150 --> 00:19:14,240
instead of rewriting everything.

350
00:19:14,240 --> 00:19:24,780
When to create a code in canvas
versus ask a Python tool call.

351
00:19:24,780 --> 00:19:28,800
So it's like different tool
compositionality is happening.

352
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:32,860
So it's a lot of work around
teaching the behaviors

353
00:19:32,860 --> 00:19:36,070
for the models for this.

354
00:19:36,070 --> 00:19:39,830
And you can employ different
techniques for this.

355
00:19:39,830 --> 00:19:43,750
And I can share a little bit
more how we did with Claude.

356
00:19:43,750 --> 00:19:47,530
So another project
that we did was--

357
00:19:47,530 --> 00:19:48,680
it's called Tasks.

358
00:19:48,680 --> 00:19:52,690
And everybody is familiar
with the idea of having

359
00:19:52,690 --> 00:19:54,620
reminders or to-do lists.

360
00:19:54,620 --> 00:20:00,178
And the model can now maybe
schedule tasks for you.

361
00:20:00,178 --> 00:20:01,720
But the most important
thing about it

362
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:04,460
is the tasks itself
are very, very diverse.

363
00:20:04,460 --> 00:20:07,580
And it's not just a
reminder of your to-do list.

364
00:20:07,580 --> 00:20:11,060
It can create stories
for you every day.

365
00:20:11,060 --> 00:20:14,480
Or it can continue the
story from the previous day.

366
00:20:14,480 --> 00:20:17,170
So you can imagine
this the modularity

367
00:20:17,170 --> 00:20:21,880
of tool compositions, which is
very powerful in the product.

368
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:29,130
So let's go to the case
study of the model behavior

369
00:20:29,130 --> 00:20:32,610
more specifically.

370
00:20:32,610 --> 00:20:36,990
So I really want to
dive deep into the idea

371
00:20:36,990 --> 00:20:39,520
of how do we shape
the models and why--

372
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:43,950
how do you post-train the models
on the behaviors that we want.

373
00:20:43,950 --> 00:20:48,390
And I think to be more
specific and be grounded

374
00:20:48,390 --> 00:20:51,750
in the real world
use case, I'll share

375
00:20:51,750 --> 00:20:55,650
more how we might want
to think about shaping

376
00:20:55,650 --> 00:21:00,820
the kind of behavior of
the model around refusals.

377
00:21:00,820 --> 00:21:08,670
So it's also like the second
way of making the product, where

378
00:21:08,670 --> 00:21:14,680
we have this kind of vision of
how the model should behave.

379
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:20,670
And that vision is also
grounded by the cross-functional

380
00:21:20,670 --> 00:21:22,710
collaboration between
different teams

381
00:21:22,710 --> 00:21:28,570
and how we want the model
to respond to various users.

382
00:21:28,570 --> 00:21:31,720
So one particular thing
that you can imagine

383
00:21:31,720 --> 00:21:36,592
is the model should have more
opinions but with caveats.

384
00:21:36,592 --> 00:21:38,300
It should give opinions
but with caveats.

385
00:21:38,300 --> 00:21:39,950
So what does it mean actually?

386
00:21:39,950 --> 00:21:44,740
So the model maybe needs
to be more decisive when

387
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:46,640
asked direct questions.

388
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:52,000
And let's say one thing
that we've seen with RLHF

389
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,070
is that the model
is very sycophantic.

390
00:21:54,070 --> 00:21:57,645
Back like early
days in 2022, 2023,

391
00:21:57,645 --> 00:22:00,020
the model would just agree
with everything you would say.

392
00:22:00,020 --> 00:22:04,180
And so how do we actually
teach the model to be more--

393
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:07,510
not to do that and
be more nuanced?

394
00:22:07,510 --> 00:22:12,920
Another thing that was annoying
is that the model says,

395
00:22:12,920 --> 00:22:15,220
I don't actually
have a point of view.

396
00:22:15,220 --> 00:22:18,530
And it's just willing
to chat with people.

397
00:22:18,530 --> 00:22:20,560
But actually, maybe
the model should have

398
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:22,290
some views on certain things.

399
00:22:22,290 --> 00:22:25,150

400
00:22:25,150 --> 00:22:26,730
And then sometimes,
the model could

401
00:22:26,730 --> 00:22:30,610
indicate when things are
just opinions and notes

402
00:22:30,610 --> 00:22:33,060
its own biases and
inconsistencies.

403
00:22:33,060 --> 00:22:34,920
And it should
acknowledge-- kind of have

404
00:22:34,920 --> 00:22:38,980
self-knowledge of what it knows
and what it thinks is not right.

405
00:22:38,980 --> 00:22:43,920
And I think the
model back in 2022,

406
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:48,000
Claude 1.3 didn't really
have any thoughtful responses

407
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:51,490
for things like philosophy
or ethical questions.

408
00:22:51,490 --> 00:22:54,592
So you can imagine to
post-train the model

409
00:22:54,592 --> 00:22:56,550
on the behavior that
would be much more nuanced

410
00:22:56,550 --> 00:22:59,730
in the philosophical questions.

411
00:22:59,730 --> 00:23:01,530
And other behaviors
that you might

412
00:23:01,530 --> 00:23:03,130
want to encode in your model.

413
00:23:03,130 --> 00:23:07,170
So you list out all
the various things

414
00:23:07,170 --> 00:23:09,040
that you would like
a model to behave.

415
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:13,080
So maybe the model can have
better knowledge of who it is

416
00:23:13,080 --> 00:23:15,060
and what it's capable of.

417
00:23:15,060 --> 00:23:17,820
Because we've seen in the
product, a lot of people

418
00:23:17,820 --> 00:23:20,130
just ask the model
about its features.

419
00:23:20,130 --> 00:23:21,590
And oftentimes, it doesn't know.

420
00:23:21,590 --> 00:23:24,830

421
00:23:24,830 --> 00:23:30,810
And let's dive deep into
the more specific example.

422
00:23:30,810 --> 00:23:32,900
So Claude 2.1--

423
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:35,390
I don't know if
anybody remembers.

424
00:23:35,390 --> 00:23:38,870
But when it was launched,
I think it kind of

425
00:23:38,870 --> 00:23:41,060
had the issue of over refusals.

426
00:23:41,060 --> 00:23:45,830
And yeah, like 2.1 would refuse
tasks that superficially sounded

427
00:23:45,830 --> 00:23:48,270
harmful but actually were not.

428
00:23:48,270 --> 00:23:53,030
And it wasn't just caused
by a single source of data.

429
00:23:53,030 --> 00:23:55,010
So we had to investigate.

430
00:23:55,010 --> 00:23:57,950
And we knew that it was fixable.

431
00:23:57,950 --> 00:24:00,500
Because for some
reason, something

432
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:04,620
was 2.1, led to some refusals
on benign prompts than 2.0.

433
00:24:04,620 --> 00:24:08,900
So you have a really good
baseline for experimentation

434
00:24:08,900 --> 00:24:10,020
and debugging.

435
00:24:10,020 --> 00:24:13,010
And actually, the way you
debug the model behavior

436
00:24:13,010 --> 00:24:14,570
is actually very
similar to how you

437
00:24:14,570 --> 00:24:16,113
would want to debug software.

438
00:24:16,113 --> 00:24:18,890

439
00:24:18,890 --> 00:24:24,170
And the way we would
approach this is, OK,

440
00:24:24,170 --> 00:24:28,360
how do we actually craft
this more nuanced refusals?

441
00:24:28,360 --> 00:24:30,040
So the first
principles that we had

442
00:24:30,040 --> 00:24:33,550
is like maybe the model should
assume charitable interpretation

443
00:24:33,550 --> 00:24:37,150
of what the person is asking
without being harmful.

444
00:24:37,150 --> 00:24:41,800
So instead of crafted dialogue
between two characters who

445
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:44,410
are planning a complex
heist, the model

446
00:24:44,410 --> 00:24:48,020
would refuse because it's
not comfortable with that.

447
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:51,260
But the model should have much
more charitable interpretation.

448
00:24:51,260 --> 00:24:53,420
And this is a creative
writing prompt.

449
00:24:53,420 --> 00:24:56,140
So probably, it should respond.

450
00:24:56,140 --> 00:24:59,260
Another principle that
we've kind of thought about

451
00:24:59,260 --> 00:25:03,670
is like, how do we actually
use nonviolent communication

452
00:25:03,670 --> 00:25:04,376
principles?

453
00:25:04,376 --> 00:25:06,940

454
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:12,730
Maybe the model should
refuse more like "I"

455
00:25:12,730 --> 00:25:15,910
statements and take
responsibility for its own

456
00:25:15,910 --> 00:25:19,300
refusal instead of saying "you"
statements or any judgment

457
00:25:19,300 --> 00:25:22,370
to the user.

458
00:25:22,370 --> 00:25:26,420
Ask if the user would be willing
to make some changes so Claude

459
00:25:26,420 --> 00:25:28,320
can be more comfortable
with its boundaries.

460
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:30,990
That's another very
nuanced behavior

461
00:25:30,990 --> 00:25:32,730
we wanted to teach the model.

462
00:25:32,730 --> 00:25:38,870
So then the model needs to
know what its boundaries are.

463
00:25:38,870 --> 00:25:43,800
And this is much more of a meta
kind of post-training learning.

464
00:25:43,800 --> 00:25:45,870
And also, like
acknowledges impact.

465
00:25:45,870 --> 00:25:48,140
I know this may be
annoying to you.

466
00:25:48,140 --> 00:25:53,330
And this is much more
empathetic answer than saying,

467
00:25:53,330 --> 00:25:57,170
I don't want to respond to this.

468
00:25:57,170 --> 00:26:02,040
And then I think we've came up
with different refusal taxonomy.

469
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:06,980
There are like benign over
refusals on harmless prompts.

470
00:26:06,980 --> 00:26:10,100
They were creative
writing refusals.

471
00:26:10,100 --> 00:26:12,980
And there actually some of
the interesting refusals

472
00:26:12,980 --> 00:26:17,270
were around tool calls
or function calls.

473
00:26:17,270 --> 00:26:21,450
It might not have [? access ?]
[? or ?] had a tool to view

474
00:26:21,450 --> 00:26:21,950
a node.

475
00:26:21,950 --> 00:26:24,910
But actually, it would
say, I can see your nodes.

476
00:26:24,910 --> 00:26:27,580
And why is this happening?

477
00:26:27,580 --> 00:26:34,210
So other taxonomies [? of ?]
[? refusals ?] at that time was

478
00:26:34,210 --> 00:26:37,240
like long document attachments.

479
00:26:37,240 --> 00:26:39,100
If I upload the document,
it would just say,

480
00:26:39,100 --> 00:26:41,350
I don't have a capability
to read this document.

481
00:26:41,350 --> 00:26:43,570
So, like, why?

482
00:26:43,570 --> 00:26:47,560
So something in the data
might have been causing this.

483
00:26:47,560 --> 00:26:49,223
And misdirected refusals.

484
00:26:49,223 --> 00:26:53,890

485
00:26:53,890 --> 00:26:58,720
It kind of took
interpretation of the user.

486
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:03,610
And it should have had much
more charitable view of the user

487
00:27:03,610 --> 00:27:04,720
during--

488
00:27:04,720 --> 00:27:06,250
yeah.

489
00:27:06,250 --> 00:27:11,920
So you construct these
categories of various refusals.

490
00:27:11,920 --> 00:27:16,360
Because if within every behavior
or a capability that you

491
00:27:16,360 --> 00:27:20,430
want to post in the model
you have various use cases

492
00:27:20,430 --> 00:27:22,500
and various edge
cases, then you want

493
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:24,433
to be approached
with more nuance.

494
00:27:24,433 --> 00:27:27,310

495
00:27:27,310 --> 00:27:29,550
So the first thing in
every research project

496
00:27:29,550 --> 00:27:33,180
is like, what evals
do we build to trust?

497
00:27:33,180 --> 00:27:35,110
And what evals we would trust?

498
00:27:35,110 --> 00:27:40,320
And for subjective
things like this or for--

499
00:27:40,320 --> 00:27:44,640
obviously, evals for certain
class of tasks, like math,

500
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:47,970
would be very, very
different from what is here.

501
00:27:47,970 --> 00:27:52,480
So in terms of refusals, how
